WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:27.080
Spartacus: The Slave Who Made Rome Tremble
According to legend, Spartacus’s wife walked

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.910
in on her sleeping husband one night to find
that a snake had wrapped itself around his

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:31.910
face.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.399
Instead of being alarmed, she took this as
a prophecy.

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:39.110
Just like the serpent surrounded itself around
the warrior’s head, so shall Spartacus be

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:42.370
enveloped by a “great and fearful power”.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:47.230
One thing the woman could not see, however,
was if this power would lead her husband to

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.320
glory or disaster.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.570
In the end, you will have to decide for yourself
what the outcome was.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:58.070
Spartacus might have lost the war, sure, but
not before making the all-powerful Roman Republic

00:00:58.070 --> 00:00:59.760
tremble at his feet.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.670
At a time when the might of Rome was continuously
expanding all over the Mediterranean, a mere

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.900
slave led an uprising the likes of which the
world had never seen.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.110
He defeated wave after wave of Roman armies
and looked, for a while at least, powerful

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.570
enough to march on Rome itself.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.720
His actions turned Spartacus into much more
than a warrior.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.410
He became a symbol in the fight against oppression.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:26.430
A symbol which is still pervasive today, over
2,000 years later.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:31.770
They say that history is written by the victors
so, in that case, Spartacus’s omen was,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.030
indeed, one of glory.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.690
Nobody mentions the success of Crassus in
putting down a slave uprising.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:43.130
Instead, they talk about the triumph and the
bravery of Spartacus.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:50.310
Early Years
Unsurprisingly, details are scarce regarding

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.280
Spartacus’s life before he rose up and became
the bane of Rome.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.790
Even the particulars we do know cannot be
taken for granted because different ancient

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.530
sources give varying accounts.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.030
Image suggestion: Spartacus statue
Everyone seems to agree that Spartacus was

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.760
a Thracian born sometime around the year 111
BCE.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.920
Plutarch, one of the main sources for the
“War of Spartacus”, as he called it, referred

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.790
to the warrior as having “nomadic stock”
and described him as being sagacious and culturally

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:17.260
superior, practically “more Hellenic than
Thracian”.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.340
Some modern scholars have speculated that
the word “nomadic” was a mistranslation

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.900
made later and that Plutarch, actually, referred
to Spartacus of being of “Maedi stock”.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.570
The Maedi were a Thracian tribe from those
times which existed in modern-day Bulgaria.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.280
Eventually, Spartacus ended up fighting in
the Roman army as an auxiliary unit.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.771
At one point, the Thracian earned the scorn
of the Romans who captured him and sold him

00:02:39.771 --> 00:02:40.771
into slavery.

00:02:40.771 --> 00:02:46.341
Again, sources vary as to the reason why - some
say that Spartacus deserted, others that he

00:02:46.341 --> 00:02:54.200
led raiding parties, or, perhaps, both.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.950
Life as a Slave
As a slave, Spartacus caught the eye of one

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:03.790
Lentulus Batiatus, a Roman who owned a gladiator
school called a “ludus” in Capua.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:08.410
He was always on the lookout for strong Gauls
and Thracians who could shed some blood for

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:09.410
the pleasure of the crowd.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.940
Image suggestion: Murmillo
In the arena, Spartacus took on the role of

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.930
a heavyweight gladiator called a “murmillo”.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.790
The look of this warrior is a Hollywood favorite
and will be familiar to anyone who saw or

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.250
played anything set in gladiator times.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:24.790
The murmillo wore a full bronze helmet and
heavy arm, leg, or shoulder guards.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.980
However, he kept his chest bare which often
allowed warriors to display the tattoos or

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.160
battle scars they accrued over the years.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:34.730
The gladiator typically wielded a large shield
called a “scutum” and a straight broadsword

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:35.730
called a “gladius”.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.000
Although Spartacus has always been depicted
in movies and TV shows as a prodigious fighter,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.440
we actually do not know anything about his
success in the arena.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.180
Since these battles were usually to the death,
he was, presumably, skilled enough to survive

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.990
until the uprising started.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:53.420
Either that or he was entertaining enough
to convince the crowd to spare him, even if

00:03:53.420 --> 00:04:00.520
he lost.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.490
The Start of the Uprising
In the year 73 BCE, Spartacus was part of

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.349
a plot to escape Batiatus’s gladiator school.

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:11.210
The number differs - Plutarch says 78 slaves
armed themselves with utensils from the kitchen,

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.120
defeated the guards and made a run for it.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.720
During their escape, they ran into carts full
of gladiator weapons being sent to another

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:17.720
city.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.130
They plundered the wagons and armed themselves
properly.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:24.820
The former slaves decided to seek refuge on
Mount Vesuvius which offered a stronger defensive

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:25.820
position.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.490
On their way there, they recruited other slaves,
as well as freemen who wanted to join their

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.490
cause.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:34.530
Ancient sources never really made it clear
what exactly Spartacus did or said to the

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:37.710
other slaves to convince them that he should
be their leader.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.120
One factor which certainly played a part was
the fact that the men split all their plunder

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.120
equally.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.090
But it should be mentioned that historians
also named two Gauls - Crixus and Oenomaus

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.020
- as leaders of the revolt.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.240
While some described them as being the equals
of Spartacus, others like Appian referred

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.710
to them as subordinate officers.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:03.040
The Battle of Mount Vesuvius
It is fair to say that when the Roman Senate

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.620
heard about Spartacus and his little uprising,
they did not take it too seriously.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.510
We may mock them for it now but, back then,
this made some sense.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:15.460
Rome had quite a few other problems on its
hands that required its attention and, more

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.290
importantly, its soldiers.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.780
In the Iberian Peninsula, Rome was engaged
in the Sertorian War against Quintus Sertorius,

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:26.680
a former Roman statesman who chose the losing
side during the Second Civil War of the Roman

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.290
Republic, but refused to concede defeat.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:33.150
Over in Asia Minor, Rome was fighting the
Third Mithridatic War against the Kingdom

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.980
of Pontus led by Mithridates VI.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:40.210
This eventually resulted in a decisive victory
for Rome and the end of the Pontic Empire,

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:44.199
but not until ten more years of vicious battles.

00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:47.160
As you can see, Rome had its plate full.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.669
Therefore, the revolt of the gladiators was
dismissed as nothing more but a crime wave

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:53.639
that could be solved with a cohort or two.

00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:59.490
In charge of this force was a praetor named
Gaius Claudius Glaber of which we know almost

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:00.490
nothing.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.919
His name suggests he could have had some kind
of distant connection with the influential

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:09.740
Claudii family, but, really, his only appearance
in the history record is his battle against

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:10.770
Spartacus.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.680
He is never mentioned again - whether this
means he died in battle or he never did anything

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:16.910
else of note, we just don’t know.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:21.790
Anyway, Glaber’s army consisted of approximately
3,000 militia, not proper Roman legionaries.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:26.210
Again, the fact that the Senate sent an unproven
military commander leading untrained militia

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:29.310
revealed how little they were concerned with
this uprising.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:33.710
Glaber and his units chased the gladiators
to their camp on Mount Vesuvius and laid siege

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:35.550
on the only road down the mountain.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:39.170
His plan was to simply wait for the insurgents
to starve to death.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:43.120
It seemed like a fine strategy, albeit a tad
unoriginal.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:44.210
Spartacus did not have this problem.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:47.199
In fact, his side deployed a much more novel
game plan.

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:51.759
He knew that Glaber was only paying attention
to the side with the descent because all the

00:06:51.759 --> 00:06:55.210
others consisted of steep cliffs which were,
seemingly, impassable.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:59.710
However, he and his men made rope ladders
out of wild vines that were plentiful in the

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.550
area and rappelled down undetected by the
Romans.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:06.900
They then surrounded Glaber’s camp and easily
defeated his forces in a surprise attack.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.040
In the end, all this expedition managed to
do was to supply the rebels with better weapons

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.850
and armor and to help bolster their numbers
as more people heard of their exploits.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:24.020
The Second Expedition
On the second attempt, Rome sent another praetor

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:27.100
named either Publius Varinius or Publius Valerius.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.540
There are no juicy details to share regarding
these fights.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.180
Just the fact that Spartacus and his men triumphed
again.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.360
First they defeated one of the praetor’s
lieutenants named Furius who had two thousand

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.360
soldiers with him.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:42.080
Then, another praetor called Cossinius who
had been sent out with another large force

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:43.650
to offer support to Varinius.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:47.490
He was almost taken prisoner by the insurgents
as the gladiators caught his camp off-guard

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.050
while the praetor was bathing.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:53.440
Lastly, the rebels defeated Varinius and,
as a symbol of his victory, Spartacus took

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:54.440
the praetor’s horse.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:58.761
Now, the gladiator army was free to raid the
countryside of Italy unopposed as more and

00:07:58.761 --> 00:08:01.080
more men from the region swelled their ranks.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.160
According to Appian, Spartacus’s army boasted
over 70,000 soldiers.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:09.790
Presumably, Oenomaus died around this time,
possibly in battle, as he is no longer mentioned

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:11.229
by historians.

00:08:11.229 --> 00:08:14.759
Spartacus and Crixus are referred to as the
commanders of the rebel army.

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:19.180
At this point, the insurgents reached a possible
crossroads in their journey, one which still

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:21.770
divides both classical and modern scholars.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:25.840
According to Plutarch, Spartacus wanted to
traverse the Alps and escape Roman territory.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.419
In his view, the Thracian planned to disband
the army once it had secured freedom.

00:08:30.419 --> 00:08:33.899
However, Spartacus could not convince his
men who were feeling cocky enough that they

00:08:33.899 --> 00:08:35.679
wanted to continue raiding.

00:08:35.679 --> 00:08:41.019
Other sources claimed that the plan was (and
had always been) to march on Rome.

00:08:41.019 --> 00:08:44.860
The notion that there was some dissension
in the ranks regarding their next move was

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:49.720
strengthened by the fact that, at one point,
the rebels split into two armies - one was

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:52.019
led by Spartacus, the other by Crixus.

00:08:52.019 --> 00:08:56.730
Whether this division was strategic so they
could cover more ground or it was caused by

00:08:56.730 --> 00:09:45.679
disagreements, we cannot say with any certainty.

00:09:45.679 --> 00:10:07.119
The Death of Crixus
By

00:10:07.119 --> 00:10:25.110
this point, the Senate realized that the little
uprising caused by the escaped slaves was

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:28.130
no laughing matter and it was only growing
in power.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:32.209
Some have argued that the Roman leaders were
reticent to unleash the true might of Rome

00:10:32.209 --> 00:10:35.129
because it would have been an admittance of
defeat, in of itself.

00:10:35.129 --> 00:10:39.750
Just the idea that a few enslaved Thracians,
Germans, and Gauls could threaten the Roman

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.959
Republic was an embarrassment.

00:10:41.959 --> 00:10:45.720
But after a year of the rebels running wild,
the Senate had had enough.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:51.949
In 72 BC, it tasked two consuls, Gellius and
Lentulus, to put an end to the uprising, and

00:10:51.949 --> 00:10:54.149
each one commanded a Roman legion.

00:10:54.149 --> 00:10:57.989
The exact size of a legion actually varied
throughout the history of Rome.

00:10:57.989 --> 00:11:01.389
According to Livy, during the Republic, the
Senate set the size at the start of the year

00:11:01.389 --> 00:11:03.709
based on availability and circumstances.

00:11:03.709 --> 00:11:09.040
Although typically a legion contained around
4,000 infantry and a few hundred cavalry,

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.850
it would vary anywhere between 3,000 and 6,000
soldiers.

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:17.240
Regardless of how many men the legions had,
they were still vastly outnumbered by the

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:18.240
insurgent forces.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.189
However, what they lacked in manpower, they
more than made up for with strict military

00:11:22.189 --> 00:11:24.339
training and top-of-the-line equipment.

00:11:24.339 --> 00:11:29.480
Spartacus’s forces, on the other hand, consisted
mostly of peasants, deserters, slaves, and

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:30.480
shepherds.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.619
Very few of them stood a chance against a
Roman soldier in combat.

00:11:33.619 --> 00:11:37.369
Image suggestion: Roman tortoise formation
Here we arrive at a bit of a contentious moment

00:11:37.369 --> 00:11:42.459
because the two main sources, Plutarch and
Appian, give differing accounts on the battles

00:11:42.459 --> 00:11:44.889
between Spartacus and the consular armies.

00:11:44.889 --> 00:11:49.779
They do not necessarily contradict each other,
but they each mention events that the other

00:11:49.779 --> 00:11:50.779
ignores.

00:11:50.779 --> 00:11:53.810
As I said previously, the rebel forces split
into two armies.

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:58.970
Both historians agree that Gellius and his
legion fought Crixus who commanded around

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:02.980
30,000 men near Mount Garganus.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:05.999
This encounter resulted in a decisive victory
for the Romans.

00:12:05.999 --> 00:12:10.250
The rebel army lost around two thirds of its
troops, including Crixus who was killed in

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:11.250
battle.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:16.879
When hearing of this, Spartacus allegedly
sacrificed 300 Roman soldiers in his honor.

00:12:16.879 --> 00:12:23.290
Fighting the Consular Armies
Both sources also agree that Spartacus and

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:28.019
the main insurgent force faced the army of
Lentulus while heading for Cisalpine Gaul

00:12:28.019 --> 00:12:29.019
and triumphed.

00:12:29.019 --> 00:12:31.429
Afterwards, however, they start to differ.

00:12:31.429 --> 00:12:35.610
We have to remember that even the best sources
we have for the War of Spartacus wrote their

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:39.709
accounts over 150 years after the uprising
had taken place.

00:12:39.709 --> 00:12:42.600
Many details could have been altered or forgotten.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:47.350
According to Appian, Gellius and his men headed
towards Spartacus after defeating Crixus.

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:51.799
His hope was that he would reach the Spartacan
army in time and trap it between the two legions

00:12:51.799 --> 00:12:54.589
commanded by him and Lentulus.

00:12:54.589 --> 00:12:58.829
He was too late, though, and the Thracian
had time to best the other consul, turn his

00:12:58.829 --> 00:13:01.470
soldiers around and defeat Gellius in combat,
also.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.939
The two consular legions retreated and regrouped
back in Rome.

00:13:04.939 --> 00:13:09.189
They then united as a single army and made
a third attempt against Spartacus at Picenum.

00:13:09.189 --> 00:13:11.410
They were defeated, once again.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:16.299
Plutarch makes no mention of Spartacus’s
fight against Gellius or the Battle at Picenum

00:13:16.299 --> 00:13:17.600
against both consuls.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:22.170
Instead, he says that, after the Thracian
bested Lentulus, he engaged in combat with

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:28.379
an army of 10,000 men led by Gaius Cassius
Longinus, the governor of Cisalpine Gaul.

00:13:28.379 --> 00:13:30.209
Once again, he was victorious.

00:13:30.209 --> 00:13:33.920
There is also some confusion regarding what
happened after Spartacus defeated the consular

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:34.920
armies.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:39.019
While Plutarch offers no concrete information,
Appian, more or less, places Spartacus on

00:13:39.019 --> 00:13:40.100
the warpath to Rome.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:44.481
Maybe it was the death of Crixus, or maybe
he thought himself invincible in battle, but

00:13:44.481 --> 00:13:47.999
Spartacus seemed determined to pillage mighty
Rome itself.

00:13:47.999 --> 00:13:52.579
He killed all his prisoners and his pack animals
and set fire to any useless materials that

00:13:52.579 --> 00:13:54.470
might have burdened and slowed him down.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:58.410
He marched on the capital with an army of
120,000 footmen.

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:02.109
Despite his gung-ho attitude, Spartacus had
a change of heart on the way and realized

00:14:02.109 --> 00:14:04.689
that, perhaps, his army was not strong enough
just yet.

00:14:04.689 --> 00:14:09.589
Instead, he took over the city of Thurii and
the surrounding mountains and conducted raids

00:14:09.589 --> 00:14:13.970
for bronze and iron to forge weapons and equip
his troops.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:21.139
Marcus Licinius Crassus
The Senate dropped any pretense that the Spartacan

00:14:21.139 --> 00:14:25.230
rebellion was anything else than a major threat
that endangered the Republic itself.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:29.640
It gave the task of putting down the uprising
once and for all to Marcus Licinius Crassus.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:32.929
Image suggestion: Bust of Crassus
Crassus is primarily remembered today for,

00:14:32.929 --> 00:14:35.459
allegedly, being the richest man in Roman
history.

00:14:35.459 --> 00:14:38.929
The true extent of his wealth is impossible
to gauge with accuracy, but it could have

00:14:38.929 --> 00:14:41.519
been up to $20 billion in modern currency.

00:14:41.519 --> 00:14:46.690
The number most commonly used is 200 million
sestertii, an estimation provided by Pliny.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:52.109
Plutarch used gold talents to quantify Crassus’s
wealth and placed it at over 7,100 talents

00:14:52.109 --> 00:14:57.129
which would mean, in modern terms, 213 metric
tons of gold.

00:14:57.129 --> 00:15:01.180
Crassus’s fortune came from “fire and
war, making the public calamities his greatest

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:03.279
source of revenue”, as Plutarch put it.

00:15:03.279 --> 00:15:06.839
Some of his lucrative businesses included
a fire department that arrived at a burning

00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:11.709
home and only extinguished the flames if it
negotiated a generous bargain with the owner.

00:15:11.709 --> 00:15:14.869
Otherwise, the men simply looked on as the
home burned to the ground.

00:15:14.869 --> 00:15:18.970
Afterwards, Crassus even offered to buy the
property (at a significantly-reduced price,

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:19.970
of course).

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:22.220
Another one of his schemes was a school for
slaves.

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:26.309
By taking cheap, unskilled labor and giving
them training, he increased their value significantly

00:15:26.309 --> 00:15:28.720
and then sold them on for a tidy profit.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:31.389
The bulk of Crassus’s wealth, however, came
from real estate.

00:15:31.389 --> 00:15:35.660
He was an ally of Sulla, the Roman general
who prompted a Civil War that involved the

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.359
aforementioned Quintus Sertorius.

00:15:37.359 --> 00:15:42.629
After his triumph, Sulla enacted proscriptions,
meaning that all of his powerful enemies were

00:15:42.629 --> 00:15:47.370
either put to death or banished and their
possessions confiscated and put up at auction.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:51.119
This allowed Crassus to purchase massive amounts
of land and property for next to nothing.

00:15:51.119 --> 00:15:55.949
Going back to the war, Crassus was not only
rich, but also had high political aspirations.

00:15:55.949 --> 00:15:59.619
He knew that putting down this rebellion would
secure his position in the Senate.

00:15:59.619 --> 00:16:02.509
He used his own money to equip and train new
troops.

00:16:02.509 --> 00:16:07.449
He was made a praetor and was granted command
of six new legions, plus what was left of

00:16:07.449 --> 00:16:08.629
the two consular armies.

00:16:08.629 --> 00:16:11.029
The Decimation
At first, it looked like this military expedition

00:16:11.029 --> 00:16:12.999
would end like all the others.

00:16:12.999 --> 00:16:16.600
While Crassus and the bulk of his army waited
for Spartacus on the borders of Picenum, he

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:21.629
sent his legate, Mummius, with two legions
to circle around and approach the rebellion

00:16:21.629 --> 00:16:22.779
army from behind.

00:16:22.779 --> 00:16:28.130
Crassus gave explicit orders not to attack
the insurgents or even to engage in skirmishes.

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:31.550
Mummius disobeyed, feeling like he had an
advantageous position.

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:33.529
He attacked and he lost.

00:16:33.529 --> 00:16:38.279
Clearly, Crassus needed to teach some discipline,
something he accomplished with an extreme

00:16:38.279 --> 00:16:39.279
measure.

00:16:39.279 --> 00:16:43.569
He revived a punishment that had not been
used for almost 200 years - decimation.

00:16:43.569 --> 00:16:47.949
The soldiers were divided into decades - a
decade, here, simply meaning a group of ten.

00:16:47.949 --> 00:16:51.999
They drew lots and the losing soldier from
every decade was executed.

00:16:51.999 --> 00:16:55.779
Again, there is a bit of disagreement over
who Crassus decimated, exactly.

00:16:55.779 --> 00:16:59.709
Plutarch said it was only one cohort of 500
or so soldiers.

00:16:59.709 --> 00:17:04.430
Appian, however, believed it was either the
two consular legions or his entire army after

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:05.430
a defeat.

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:08.300
This is a pretty big detail because it makes
the difference between the execution of 50

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:11.240
soldiers, more or less, and the execution
of thousands.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:15.780
Either way, the tactic seemed to work, and
Crassus showed his men that he was “more

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:19.020
dangerous...than the enemy”.

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:25.630
Victories for Both Sides
Crassus’s methods might have been harsh,

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:26.800
but they yielded results.

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:30.880
The following times that the armies met in
battle, the Romans were victorious.

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:34.510
Spartacus was forced to retreat to a region
in southern Italy called Lucania.

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:38.390
Plutarch reports that the Thracian planned
to take over the island of Sicily and regroup

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:39.390
there.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:42.400
For this, he negotiated passage with a group
of Cilician pirates.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:46.260
However, with pirates being pirates, they
double-crossed him and sailed away after being

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:47.260
paid.

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:50.940
Spartacus had his back against the wall so
he fell back to the peninsula of Rhegium.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:55.600
Crassus felt that he had victory in his grasp,
but he was wise enough not to underestimate

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:58.070
the Thracian as many had done before him.

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:02.340
Instead of forcing a battle, he built fortifications
across the isthmus to the peninsula so that

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:04.390
the rebels would be cut off from any provisions.

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:07.290
Indeed, this tactic split the rebellious fighters
into two groups.

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:11.110
One of them broke through the siege and started
their own raiding campaign.

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:14.890
Crassus fell upon them swiftly and destroyed
them in battle, but he was disheartened to

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:17.770
see that their fighting spirit had not been
extinguished.

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:22.220
Although he killed over 12,000 insurgents,
upon inspection, only two of them had wounds

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:23.220
in their backs.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:27.720
All the others had died facing their enemy,
fighting until their last breath.

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:32.250
Meanwhile, Crassus sent another army led by
one of his officers, Quintus, to pursue the

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:34.770
Spartacan forces which fled to the mountains.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:39.460
The gladiator turned his troops around and
met the Romans in battle and was triumphant.

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:44.130
Bolstered by this recent victory, the rebels
wanted to take on the main army of Crassus.

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:52.240
The Arrival of Pompey
One final, decisive battle suited Crassus

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:53.320
just fine.

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:57.680
Although at first he seemed content with weakening
the insurgents as much as possible, now he

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:02.470
was fighting against the clock as he received
news that Roman reinforcements were on the

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:03.470
way.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:06.070
Pompey the Great had managed to put down the
Sertorian rebellion in the Iberian Peninsula

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:09.030
and had redirected his forces to take on Spartacus.

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:12.390
Another commander named Lucullus was also
coming with additional soldiers.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:16.180
You would think that the arrival of reinforcements
would be good news for Crassus, but he had

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:20.290
no intention of sharing credit with anyone
for putting down the largest rebellion in

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:21.310
Roman history.

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:25.730
Before making his last stand, Spartacus actually
tried to call a truce with Crassus, but the

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:27.500
Roman praetor rejected it categorically.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:32.110
To fulfill his ambitions, nothing but the
absolute obliteration of the revolt would

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:33.110
have sufficed.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:35.830
The armies met at the Battle of the Silarius
River.

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:39.260
The Spartacan troops fought hard, but were
soundly defeated.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:41.350
Around 36,000 of them died in battle.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:46.590
Around 6,000 more were taken prisoner by Crassus
and were all crucified along the Appian Way.

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:51.040
About 5,000 rebels managed to flee the battle,
but they were met by Pompey who cut them to

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:52.040
pieces.

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:53.040
Image suggestion: Appian Way
Triumph

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:56.381
Pompey told Rome that, even though Crassus
won the battle, he “extirpated the war”.

00:19:56.381 --> 00:20:00.320
Accordingly, for this and his victory in Hispania,
he was given a major triumph upon his return

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:01.950
and made consul.

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:05.190
In this case, a triumph is a Roman ritual
which celebrates a general’s successes in

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:06.190
battle.

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:08.940
Crassus was later made a censor with Pompey’s
support.

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:13.510
He also won over the masses of Rome by using
his money to throw a giant feast in honor

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:17.210
of Hercules and paying for everyone’s grain
allowance for three months.

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:21.250
The two of them later formed an informal alliance
called the First Triumvirate alongside another

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:23.800
Roman up-and-comer named Julius Caesar.

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:30.850
The Fate of Spartacus
It would have been nice if we could have ended

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:36.040
with one final anecdote about Spartacus since
this bio is about him, after all.

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:38.350
Unfortunately, history is seldom that accommodating.

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:43.300
He died in battle - killed at an unknown time
by an unknown soldier - and his body was never

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:44.300
recovered.

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:48.150
Even though he had no possible way of knowing
this, Plutarch thought to provide a more fitting

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:51.150
end for the Thracian who tried to bring Rome
to its knees.

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:55.330
He claimed that, during battle, Spartacus
cut his way through the enemy ranks and tried

00:20:55.330 --> 00:21:00.230
to reach Crassus himself, but was stopped
when two dead centurions fell upon him.

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:20.530
He was then completely surrounded and cut
down while fighting to the bitter end.

